# EDITOR — commits and error fixes

**Load only after SKILL.md confirmed the role is EDITOR.**

Scope: claim an upstream issue, fix the code in a worktree, gate it with modum,
push, hand off branch + sha. The Editor NEVER dispatches CI, NEVER watches runs,
and NEVER touches binaries — that is all Compiler territory.

## Phase 0 — Fresh base

```bash
git -C ~/opencrabs fetch adolfousier main && git -C ~/opencrabs merge --ff-only adolfousier/main
```

- Branch off fresh main; never off a stale checkout.
- Before building on an existing branch: diff it against its merge-base to confirm no
  foreign WIP rode along from parallel agents. Take a backup branch ref before any
  `rebase --onto`. *(learned 2026-08-25: branch carried another agent's dropped WIP)*

## Phase 1 — Claim on upstream BEFORE editing

1. Search existing issues first: `gh search issues ... -R adolfousier/opencrabs`.
2. None fits → open ONE issue with symptom + evidence (error text, run link, sha).
3. Comment **"Tackling this — `<branch name>`"** so every other agent sees it's taken.
   Re-check comments right before starting; someone may have claimed while you read.

## Phase 2 — Worktree per task, before any edits

```bash
git -C ~/opencrabs worktree add ~/oc-wt-<task> <branch>
```

ALL edits happen in the worktree, never in the shared checkout. One task = one
worktree = one branch. Parallel agents share the repo; the shared checkout can be
switched under you mid-task at any moment.

Before adding: `git -C ~/opencrabs worktree list` — prune stale entries first
(`git worktree prune`); never reuse another live task's path.

## Phase 3 — Explore before writing

- **Structure, callers, impact chains:** `grep_code` (codegraph) — who calls this,
  what else breaks when it changes.
- **Library APIs and version behavior:** `grep_docs` (Context7) — verify the method
  EXISTS and which trait provides it BEFORE using it. *(teloxide setters are
  per-payload traits: import every trait whose method you call)*
- **Duplication check (DRY):** assume a helper already exists — find it before
  writing a new one. Reuse beats re-implement.
- **Module size:** prefer extracting a NEW module over growing any file past
  ~1000 lines.

## Phase 4 — Shape the change

- One logical change per branch; drive-by refactors go to their own branch + issue.
- Stage only paths YOU changed: `git add <paths>`. Never `git add -A`, never `commit -a`.
- Never revert/reset/amend commits you did not write — report and wait, or branch off.

## Phase 5 — modum gate before every commit

```bash
cd ~/oc-wt-<task> && modum lint .
```

Zero errors required. Pre-existing warnings in untouched files ≠ blocker; any finding
in YOUR changed lines = fix first, then commit. (Invocation takes root/glob flags,
not a bare filename.)

## Phase 6 — Push and hand off

```bash
git -C ~/oc-wt-<task> push -u origin <branch>
```

Report to the ops chat: branch name + pushed sha. That hand-off ENDS the Editor's
part. Dispatching, watching the run, verifying headSha, reading conclusions — ALL
Compiler work; the Editor does none of it.

Triage heuristics below stay HERE because they are what you apply when the Compiler
hands a red run back with evidence:

- Fix unresolved-name/import errors first (E0425/E0433...) — later errors are
  usually poisoned fallout. When scopes look shifted, count brace DEPTH, not brace
  counts.
- Match-arm narrowing does not inherit through outer arms — an inner match needs its
  own exhaustive arms regardless of the outer guard.
- Red run returned → recreate the worktree if removed (see below) → fix on the SAME
  branch → commit → push → report the NEW sha. The Compiler re-dispatches.
- Once the Compiler confirms the green run, post the fix evidence to the upstream
  issue (the Editor owns the issue thread end to end).

## Worktree lifecycle — delete early, recreate on demand

The worktree's job ends the moment your code is committed AND pushed — you don't
watch CI at all. CI compiles on GitHub, not here; nothing merges anywhere (upstream
is issues-only — branches stay on the fork as archive).

DELETE immediately after a verified clean push:

```bash
git -C ~/oc-wt-<task> status --porcelain   # must be empty — all committed & pushed
git -C ~/opencrabs worktree remove ~/oc-wt-<task>
```

RECREATE only when the Compiler reports the run RED and fixes are needed — same branch, fresh
worktree, continue from Phase 3/5 as applicable:

```bash
git -C ~/opencrabs worktree add ~/oc-wt-<task> <branch>
```

Never leave orphaned trees behind: check `git worktree list` before starting any new
task; `git worktree prune` cleans stale entries.
